Satellite operators in Low-Earth Orbit (LEO) are now facing an unprecedented challenge of navigating a rapidly expanding number of spacecraft as the number of commercial and military satellites continues to surge in the region. According to a recent report by the Smithsonian, more than 8,000 satellites are now in operation in LEO, up from just 500 last year, and are expected to nearly double by 2023 to reach a total of 15,000. This significant increase has led to concerns over space debris and collisions, as well as interference and incidents, prompting calls for improved safety measures and regulations in the area. A key issue in LEO is now becoming to keep track of the satellites themselves, as the following figure illustrates, showing a crowded field in the region, with many operators' spacecraft in close proximity to one another. 

