  Our proposed solution to this problem is to leverage the use of small low-cost satellites known as "cubesats" to develop innovative tools for space situational awareness, by using the numerous cubesats in orbit as test platforms for experimental work. As the following image illustrates, these cubesats are now playing a key role in providing critical space data to various agencies around the world. 

  \begin{center}\includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{cubesats-2022-06-27.png}
  \end{center}

  A particular area where these tools could be developed quickly and efficiently is in the use of cubesats to track and record the orbital paths of other satellites in the region, by using the satellites' built-in cameras and navigation systems to gather precise data on the other objects in the LEO environment. Our proposed $3^{rd}$ generation cubesat, the "AWACS" satellite, is now being designed to develop and test these tools for space situational awareness, by using the satellite's advanced imaging capabilities to track and monitor other objects in the LEO environment and providing real-time data on the other satellites in the region to various agencies around the world. Our work in this area is now providing a critical stepping stone for the development of more advanced tools for space situational awareness in the years to come. 
